INT. COUNTY FAIR - PIE TENT - DAY

A canvas tent in the August glare. Long tables of judged pies, blue ribbons pinned like medals. Flies drone. A fan turns slowly overhead.

PENELOPE "PENNY" ASHWORTH-BELL, 16, in a gingham apron two sizes too large, stands guard over a single lattice-topped peach pie. Her fingers are stained with flour and nerves.

Her grandmother, MAUDE ASHWORTH-BELL, 70s, enormous sunhat, sits on a folding chair behind her and says nothing, in the way only grandmothers can.

Down the row, JUDGE THEODORE "TEDDY" CROMWELL, 60s, seersucker suit, moves from pie to pie with a silver fork and a stern expression.

A late arrival bursts in, breathless. WILLIAM "WILLIE" TANAKA, 17, in a rumpled work shirt, grease on his cheek, carrying a pie plate wrapped in a dish towel.

WILLIE
Penny! Penny, I made it.

PENNY
Willie. The deadline was ten.

WILLIE
It's ten-oh-four.

PENNY
That's closed.

WILLIE
They can't close on a four-minute margin.

PENNY
They can. They have.

Maude lifts her chin a fraction beneath the hat.

MAUDE
Four minutes is four minutes, young man.

WILLIE
Mrs. Ashworth-Bell. I didn't see you there.

MAUDE
Few people do. It's a gift.

WILLIE
(to Penny)
I stayed up all night. I burned the first one. And the second.

PENNY
I can smell it.

WILLIE
It's the sacrifice.

She cannot help it. She laughs. Quickly claps a hand over her mouth.

Judge Cromwell reaches the end of the row and looks up, eyebrow arched.

CROMWELL
Is there a disturbance?

PENNY
No, sir.

WILLIE
Yes, sir. I'm late.

CROMWELL
Hm.

He studies the dish towel in Willie's hands. The corner has stained with something dark and sticky.

CROMWELL (CONT'D)
What have you there, son?

WILLIE
A blackberry, sir. Mostly.

CROMWELL
Mostly.

WILLIE
There was an incident with the oven.

MAUDE
(dryly)
I'll bet.

Cromwell holds out his hand. Willie hesitates, then unwraps the towel. The pie inside is lopsided, the crust charred at one edge, the filling bubbling over like lava.

Penny stares at it. Then at Willie. Then at the judge.

PENNY
Sir, he's late. By the rules, he can't--

CROMWELL
Miss Ashworth-Bell. I have judged this tent for thirty-one years. I have never once seen a pie arrive so wretchedly.

WILLIE
Thank you, sir?

CROMWELL
It was not a compliment.

He cuts a sliver with the silver fork. Lifts it. Tastes.

Everyone holds still. The fan turns. A fly lands on the nearest table and thinks better of it.

CROMWELL (CONT'D)
(slowly)
Burnt sugar. Lemon zest. A touch of black pepper.

WILLIE
My mother's trick.

CROMWELL
Hm.

He takes another bite. Then a third. Then, with a visible effort at composure, sets down the fork.

CROMWELL (CONT'D)
The rules are the rules. Your entry is disqualified.

WILLIE
Yes, sir.

PENNY
I'm sorry, Willie.

CROMWELL
However.

He turns and walks to the head table. Takes a small rosette from a tray. Not blue. Green. Pins it to the lapel of Willie's work shirt with brisk, painless precision.

CROMWELL (CONT'D)
That is the Judge's Indulgence. It is awarded perhaps once a decade.

WILLIE
(whispering)
Penny, I think I'm going to cry.

PENNY
(whispering back)
Don't. You'll get it in the pie.

CROMWELL
Miss Ashworth-Bell, your peach has a fine lattice. Competent. Tidy.

PENNY
Tidy?

CROMWELL
First place.

Penny's mouth falls open. Maude, behind her, finally smiles beneath the hat.

PENNY
(to Willie, at once with Cromwell)
You beat me by being late!

CROMWELL ^
The tent is closed to further questions.

He marches off with the silver fork. The two teenagers look at each other, unable to speak.

MAUDE
(rising, dusting her skirt)
Well. That's two ribbons and a fire hazard. Come, children. Cake.

She sweeps out. Penny and Willie follow, still grinning, still stunned, the green rosette bobbing on his chest.
